# xhci98.sys import allowlist - module/symbol pairs this driver may import.
#
# An unresolved import stops the driver before DriverEntry on both targets,
# with no call-site diagnostic (docs\usb-xhci-info\win98-wdm.md "Imports are a silent
# load-time gate"). This file is the committed decision about which pairs are
# allowed to appear in the linked binary; scripts\import-gate\check-imports.ps1
# enforces it after every link and rejects anything else.
#
# Pairs, not names. The PE import descriptor names the providing module, so
# NTOSKRNL.EXE!DbgPrint and HAL.DLL!DbgPrint are different imports and only one
# of them resolves. Module names are matched case-insensitively (the loader
# does too - the linker records "ntoskrnl.exe" and "HAL.dll" in this build but
# "NTOSKRNL.EXE" and "HAL.DLL" in the shipping NUSB miniport); symbol names are
# matched exactly and case-sensitively.
#
# USBPORT.SYS rows are deliberately absent. They are read from
# scripts\usbport-lib\usbport-imports.expected, which the .def and the import
# library are already generated from; a second hand-maintained copy of the same
# two names is how the lists silently diverge (roadmap Phase 3 task 5).
#
# Format, one entry per line, whitespace separated:
#
#   MODULE!SYMBOL   FLAVORS   REQUIREMENT   evidence notes to end of line
#
#   FLAVORS      release | debug | qemu | all - which build flavor may import it
#   REQUIREMENT  required                     - the gate fails if it is missing
#                optional                     - may be absent (build-flag
#                                               dependent)
#
# **"both" is retired and check-imports.ps1 refuses it** (task 13-L.1). It was
# this column's word for "every flavor" while there were two, and there are now
# three - release, debug and qemu - so a row written about two builds must not
# be silently read as covering three. The word is "all". A qemu row is the
# strongest statement this file can make about a published binary: qemu is
# never published, so "qemu required" means "and no shipping build may have it".
#
# Evidence tags in the last column, strongest first. check-imports.ps1
# re-derives all three when the extracted target files are present and prints
# which ones ran, so a row's claim is never taken on trust:
#
#   w2k-export         The symbol is in the export table of every Windows 2000
#                      SP4 kernel and HAL image on the media - not just the pair
#                      the 2b VM happens to run. Authoritative for Win2000:
#                      that is exactly what its loader resolves against.
#   win98-precedent    A binary that ships as part of Win98 SE or of the NUSB
#                      package installed on the 2a VM imports the same pair.
#                      Strong but indirect: it says the pair resolved for that
#                      driver on this platform, not that ours will load.
#   ntkern-name        The symbol name appears as a NUL-delimited string in
#                      Win98 SE's ntkern.vxd, which builds the NT-style export
#                      tables at init. Positive evidence only: the absence of a
#                      name there means nothing (Win98 SE's own usbd.sys
#                      imports names that are not in it - see below).
#
# The authoritative Win98 check is the load itself, on the 2a VM (roadmap
# Phase 3 task 8). Nothing host-side can replace it: on Win98 the NT-style
# export tables are built at run time by ntkern.vxd rather than read from a
# file, so there is no export table on disk to resolve against.
#
# No baseline file identities are recorded here. Each evidence source has one
# tracked manifest that both names the files and authenticates them, so there is
# no prose copy to drift out of date - a hash nobody compares is decoration:
#
#   scripts\import-gate\win2k-baselines.expected  both SP4 kernel images and all
#     eight HAL images on the media, including the SMP kernel, the ACPI HALs,
#     and the one uncompressed image a HAL*.DL_ pattern misses.
#   scripts\import-gate\win98-evidence.list       the [precedent] binaries
#     (Win98 SE's own USB 1.1 stack and the NUSB set installed on the 2a VM) and
#     the [nametable] file, ntkern.vxd.
#
# Both are verified by version, length, and SHA-256 before the gate reads a
# single export name or reports a single evidence tag.

[imports]

# --- MMIO and register-settle waits (src\xhci_pci.c) ---------------------
# Phase 4 task 2 is where this driver first touches BAR0, so these are the
# first imports the release build has ever carried beyond the two usbport exports.
# All three are the exact module/symbol pairs NUSB's own USBEHCI.SYS imports on
# Win98 - including the surprise that READ/WRITE_REGISTER_ULONG come from
# NTOSKRNL.EXE and not from HAL.DLL, which is the whole reason this file
# records pairs rather than names. On x86 the Win2K DDK declares all three as
# real imports (wdm.h's NTHALAPI block); they are macros only on Alpha/IA64.
ntoskrnl.exe!READ_REGISTER_ULONG     all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent USBEHCI.SYS (NTOSKRNL.EXE!READ_REGISTER_ULONG, hint C7)
ntoskrnl.exe!WRITE_REGISTER_ULONG    all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent USBEHCI.SYS (NTOSKRNL.EXE!WRITE_REGISTER_ULONG, hint F4)
# The busy-wait half of XhciWaitForBits. Callable at any IRQL and never used as
# a timer (docs\contributing\implementation-invariants.md, "Wait Primitives").
HAL.dll!KeStallExecutionProcessor    all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent USBEHCI.SYS (HAL.DLL!KeStallExecutionProcessor, hint 5)

# --- The miniport's interior lock (src\xhci_cmd.c) -----------------------
# Phase 4 task 7. The command engine is reached from three contexts that no
# single usbport lock covers - a submit under MiniportSpinLock, the completion
# under MiniportInterruptsSpinLock, and an uncancellable async timeout under
# neither (docs\usb-xhci-info\usbport-miniport-abi.md section 7) - so the miniport needs its
# own. The ISR is deliberately not one of them, which is what keeps this an
# ordinary DISPATCH-level spin lock rather than an interrupt spin lock (whose
# XP-era Ke* entry points are on the deny list below).
#
# These are the exact pairs the Win2K DDK's x86 declarations produce:
# KeInitializeSpinLock is NTKERNELAPI, and KeAcquireSpinLock/KeReleaseSpinLock
# are macros over the FASTCALL Kf* entry points that _DECL_HAL_KE_IMPORT puts
# in HAL.DLL. Win98 SE's *own* usbhub.sys and usbd.sys import all three, from
# those same modules, which is the strongest Win98 evidence this project has.
#
# The shipping usbehci.sys imports none of them - it keeps no state that two
# usbport locks can reach at once. This driver's command engine does, so this is
# the one place where being a strict subset of that miniport's import profile
# had to be given up, deliberately and with the reason recorded.
ntoskrnl.exe!KeInitializeSpinLock     all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent usbhub.sys 4.10.2222 (NTOSKRNL.EXE!KeInitializeSpinLock, hint 77); ntkern-name
# The command engine's start epoch. Shared across every controller this driver
# serves, and PnP can start two of them concurrently - nothing in the miniport
# ABI serializes StartController across controllers - so a plain ++ could hand
# the same token to both, which is the one thing that counter must never do.
ntoskrnl.exe!InterlockedIncrement     all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent usbhub.sys 4.10.2222 (NTOSKRNL.EXE!InterlockedIncrement, hint 28); ntkern-name
HAL.dll!KfAcquireSpinLock             all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent usbhub.sys 4.10.2222 (HAL.DLL!KfAcquireSpinLock, hint 6); ntkern-name. KeAcquireSpinLock(a,b) is the DDK's x86 macro for this.
HAL.dll!KfReleaseSpinLock             all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent usbhub.sys 4.10.2222 (HAL.DLL!KfReleaseSpinLock, hint 9); ntkern-name. KeReleaseSpinLock(a,b) is the DDK's x86 macro for this.

# --- The log ring's IRQL guard (src\xhci_dispatch.c) ---------------------
# **Task 11-V.7's three file-sink imports - ZwCreateFile, ZwWriteFile and
# ZwClose - and the block that justified them were REMOVED here by task 13-L.2
#, with the sink itself.** The reason is evidential and it is not
# a load-time one: those two names rest on `ntkern-name` alone and DO resolve on
# the E460, since the release build loads there. What retired them is that the
# file sink has never written a byte on any real machine, on either target -
# task 11-V.7 ran on the 2a and 2b guests, so both halves of its record are
# virtual-machine readings, and Windows 2000 has never run on real hardware in
# this project at all. A channel measured working only in an emulator, force
# declined on the one target that has metal, and unknown in both directions
# there, is not what 1.0.0.0 publishes. The log file did not go away with it: it
# moved to ring 3, where XHCISNAP writes it and names it from the command line.
#
# **The `ntkern-name` tier is NOT retired by their departure** and neither are
# defect 2b's two boots. The WRITE_PORT_UCHAR block below used to cite these two
# rows as its reason for caring about the tier; that consequence goes and the
# question does not - the tier is unreliable rather than wrong, and it is what
# every future Windows 98 import is judged on.
#
# One finding worth keeping out of that block: ZwSetInformationFile is one of
# the Zw* names *missing* from ntkern.vxd's table, which is why the retired
# append used FILE_APPEND_DATA plus a FILE_WRITE_TO_END_OF_FILE offset rather
# than a seek. Anyone reaching for file I/O in this driver again starts there.
# Promoted from debug-only by task 11-V.7: the flush measures its own IRQL and
# refuses above PASSIVE_LEVEL rather than resting on a static claim about which
# lifecycle callbacks are passive - a claim whose derivation was started and not
# finished (src\xhci_log.c). It outlives the file sink that motivated it: the
# flush now hands the ring to DbgPrint, and DbgPrint from DPC and ISR contexts
# is the other thing this project has measured bugchecking Windows 98 metal, so
# the guard is if anything more load-bearing than it was.
HAL.dll!KeGetCurrentIrql         all  required  w2k-export; win98-precedent NTMAP.SYS 4.10.2227 (HAL.DLL!KeGetCurrentIrql, hint 3). NOT in ntkern.vxd's name strings - that source has false negatives, see the header.
# Promoted from debug-only by task 11-V.9's `XhciLogDebugView` sink, and the
# promotion is the whole of that value's cost. **What the release build gains
# is one bulk dump of the ring from the PASSIVE flush, not the debug build's
# per-line mirroring** - it is exactly that per-line profile, from DPC and ISR
# contexts at real interrupt rates, that bugchecks Windows 98 on metal
# (0028:C208D79D and 0028:C207B26D, roadmap task 12.2), which is why the sink is
# reachable only from the flush and why live mirroring stays a build
# distinction rather than something a registry value can turn on.
#
# The Windows 98 evidence is its own and is not inherited from the debug build's
# row below: DbgPrint was measured reaching DebugView inside the 2a VM on
# in batch 7b, cross-checked against the 0xE9 sink. That the bulk dump is *safe*
# on Windows 98 **metal** is not established and is not claimed here - task 12.2
# owns the decision about a channel, and Phase 13 owns the observation if that
# decision asks for one.
ntoskrnl.exe!DbgPrint            all  required  w2k-export; ntkern-name; runtime-observed reaching DebugView in the 2a VM (cross-checked against the port-0xE9 sink)

# --- The qemu flavour's trace channel (src\xhci_dbg.c) -----------------
# Through Phase 4 task 5 the release profile was a strict *subset* of the
# shipping NUSB usbehci.sys's (which also imports NTOSKRNL.EXE!KeQuerySystemTime);
# task 7's spin lock ended that, for the reason recorded in its block above. The
# rule that replaces it is the same one in weaker form: every pair here must
# carry Win98 evidence of its own, because every addition is a new way for the
# load to fail silently on Win98.
#
# DbgPrint used to be a debug-only row here and is now required in every flavor
# by task 11-V.9's DebugView sink - see its block above.
#
# **And since task 13-L.1 that is the ONLY reason any published binary imports
# it**, because the live per-line trace this block is about is now the `qemu`
# flavour's alone. `src/xhci_dbg.c` compiles away without `XHCI_DBG_LIVE`, which
# only `qemu` defines. The reason is the same measurement in both directions:
# per-line DbgPrint from DPC and ISR contexts at real interrupt rates is what
# bugchecks Windows 98 on metal across three device classes, and `debug` is the
# flavour a user with a problem is told to install. So `debug` and `release`
# emit identically - one bulk dump from the PASSIVE flush - and what separates
# them is smaller than "checked versus free" sounds: **both compile /Oxs**, and
# this driver has no runtime asserts at all. `debug` adds /Oy- (frame
# pointers), VS_FF_DEBUG in the version resource, the snapshot header's DEBUG
# bit and the XHCI98_FLAVOUR_* marker - and nothing else. The measured compiler
# delta is -DDBG=1, -DFPO=0 against -DFPO=1, the flavour define, and /Oy- against
# /Oy; compare src/buildchk.log and src/buildfre.log, which print the line. (This comment said "the DDK's own asserts and optimisation"
# until the post-Phase 13 review rounds; both halves of that were wrong and the same sentence had
# already been written wrongly three times elsewhere. Check it against
# src/sources and src/buildchk.log, never against a comment.) (The first cut of
# the split gated only the port-0xE9 half and left this one behind; a Codex
# review of commit `efa86a3` caught it.)
#
# "Prints", not "mirrors into the log ring" - the two channels are DISJOINT and
# an earlier wording here said otherwise. XhciDbgEmit (src\xhci_dbg.c) writes
# port 0xE9 and DbgPrint and touches no ring; the ring is filled only by the
# explicit XhciLogNote producers, identically in every flavor. So the qemu build
# has a live per-event trace AS WELL AS the ring, not a copy of one inside the
# other - which is why an XHCI_DBG_* site is not evidence in a stored log.
# KeGetCurrentIrql used to be a debug-only row here, dropped by XHCI_DBG_NO_IRQL
# without touching a call site. It is now required in every flavor by task
# 11-V.7's flush guard - see its block above - so that escape hatch no longer
# removes the import, only the IRQL field of a trace line.
#
# --- The QEMU port-0xE9 mirror, and why this row is the enforcement ------
#
# **THIS ROW IS TASK 13-L.1's REPAIR.** It was `debug optional` and is now
# `qemu required`, and that single change is what turns a recorded defect into
# an enforced one: qemu is never published, so a published binary carrying this
# import now FAILS THE GATE rather than merely being noted in this file. The
# `required` half matters as much as the flavor - it is what catches a qemu
# build that quietly lost the mirror and would then have answered defect 2b's
# question about a binary that never had the import in the first place.
#
# **MEASURED AND THIS IS THE DEFECT THE ROW NOW PREVENTS.**
# The published debug flavour of 0.0.0.4 does not load on the ThinkPad E460
# (Win98 SE, NUSB 3.3): the controller devnode takes a yellow bang and Code 2,
# with no crash and nothing loaded. This pair is the **sole** import difference
# between the two 0.0.0.4 binaries - verified by DUMPBIN /IMPORTS on both - and
# a rebuild without this pair loads clean and drives real devices on that
# machine. The control was taken and it is the one that can be taken after the
# fact: restoring the previous binary cleared the bang, which implicates the
# BUILD rather than something already on the machine. It does not implicate the
# import, and that rebuild was not otherwise identical either - it was a
# diagnostic build, so it carried the do-not-deploy marker as well. (That rebuild was `XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES=-DXHCI_DBG_NO_E9`,
# which is also why it could not be shipped: any nonempty XHCI_EXTRA_DEFINES
# marks the image undeployable. The negative is retired - the mirror is now the
# opt-IN `XHCI_DBG_E9` that only the qemu flavor defines - so the binary that
# loads is the ordinary debug flavor and needs no escape hatch at all.)
#
# **Why is not established, and the two readings have different consequences:**
# either the import does not resolve there - which would make `ntkern-name`
# insufficient rather than merely incomplete, since the name IS in the SE CD's
# ntkern.vxd and that machine was installed from that CD - or port 0xE9 is
# decoded by something on that chipset and the write faults during early init,
# which would refute src\xhci_dbg.c's comment that "on real hardware nothing
# decodes that port and the byte is discarded". A clean Code 2 rather than a
# bugcheck favours the first and does not settle it.
#
# The tier is unreliable, not wrong, and this row does not retire it. That used
# to be said by pointing at ZwCreateFile and ZwWriteFile, which rested on
# `ntkern-name` alone and DO resolve on that machine since the release build
# loads there. **Task 13-L.2 removed those two rows with the file sink**, so
# that particular illustration is gone - and the question is not: `ntkern-name`
# is still the evidence tier every future Windows 98 import is judged on, and
# defect 2b's two boots are still owed.
#
# **The discriminating binaries are BUILT** and the question is
# now waiting on two boots rather than on any work. -DXHCI_DBG_E9_NOEXEC keeps
# this import - behind a file-scope volatile gate nothing ever sets - and never
# executes the write; its import table is IDENTICAL to the published debug
# flavour's, 18 symbols, verified with DUMPBIN, so the only runtime difference
# is whether the write happens. Code 2 from that binary settles it as the
# import; a clean load points at the port, pending one control boot for
# XHCI_DIAGNOSTIC_BUILD. Procedure, hashes and decision tree:
# docs\contributing\runs\run-13e.md, "P6. Build the two binaries that settle
# defect 2b".
#
# **Do not "fix" this by dropping the pair**, and do not move it back to `debug`
# to make some build convenient. The mirror is this project's primary instrument
# in QEMU, where port 0xE9 is the whole point of it, and the qemu flavour exists
# so that instrument can keep it while no published binary does. "What to publish
# for bare metal" owned no task when this block was first written; it is now task
# 13-L.1 and this row is its enforcement. Evidence:
# docs\contributing\runs\run-13e.md, "Session record - the DebugView ban, measured", finding A, and
# docs\contributing\design\08-build-flavours-and-the-log-channel.md.
HAL.dll!WRITE_PORT_UCHAR        qemu    required  w2k-export; ntkern-name; MEASURED NOT LOADING on E460 Win98 SE metal - see the block above. The qemu flavour is never published, so this row is what keeps the import out of every binary a user can install.

[deny]

# Symbols that must never appear in the binary, whatever module claims to
# provide them and whoever calls them. A denied name is reported with its
# reason instead of the generic "not in the allowlist", because each of these
# has a specific, already-diagnosed cause. Matched by symbol name alone.

ExAllocatePoolWithTag       The local Win2K DDK defines POOL_TAGGING and rewrites ExAllocatePool(a,b) into this. Undefine the macro in src\xhci_compat.h after the DDK includes and call the real two-argument ExAllocatePool. Under Option A the miniport should need no private pool at all - fixed metadata belongs in the usbport-allocated extensions (docs\contributing\design\04-controller-common-buffer.md).
ExFreePoolWithTag           Same DDK macro family as ExAllocatePoolWithTag; use ExFreePool.
MmGetSystemRoutineAddress   Missing on Win98 (Oney p.438). There is no runtime symbol resolution on that target - every import decision is static and made here.
KeNumberProcessors          Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.441). Win98 is uniprocessor by construction; per-CPU code belongs behind a Win2000-only build, not a runtime check.
IoAllocateWorkItem          Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.375). Use usbport's UsbPortRequestAsyncCallback with miniport-owned synchronization.
IoQueueWorkItem             Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.375).
IoFreeWorkItem              Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.375).
IoAcquireRemoveLock         The IO_REMOVE_LOCK family is missing on Win98 SE (Oney p.191). Keep an outstanding-I/O count instead.
IoReleaseRemoveLock         IO_REMOVE_LOCK family - missing on Win98 SE (Oney p.191).
IoReleaseRemoveLockAndWait  IO_REMOVE_LOCK family - missing on Win98 SE (Oney p.191).
MmMapLockedPagesSpecifyCache  Missing on Win98; MmGetSystemAddressForMdlSafe expands to it (Oney p.225). Option B only.
PsGetVersion                Missing on Win98 (Oney p.437). Use IoIsWdmVersionAvailable.
KeReadStateEvent            Missing on Win98 (Oney p.95).
IoReportTargetDeviceChangeAsynchronous  Not exported on Win98 - a load-blocking import, not a runtime failure (Oney p.191).
PoRegisterDeviceNotify      Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoCancelDeviceNotify        Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoRegisterSystemState       Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoSetSystemState            Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoUnregisterSystemState     Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
HalTranslateBusAddress      Only partially available on Win98 (Oney p.441). Use the translated resources PnP already supplies; under Option A usbport hands the miniport its resources.
KeAcquireInterruptSpinLock  Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000 (docs\usb-xhci-info\win98-wdm.md). Under Option A usbport owns the interrupt object.
KeReleaseInterruptSpinLock  Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000.
IoForwardIrpSynchronously   Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000. Use IoCallDriver plus a completion routine that signals a KEVENT.
IoSetCompletionRoutineEx    Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000.
IoCsqInitialize             Cancel-safe queues arrived with the XP DDK and are not in the Win2K DDK's libraries.
IoCsqInsertIrp              Cancel-safe queue family - not in the Win2K DDK.
IoCsqRemoveNextIrp          Cancel-safe queue family - not in the Win2K DDK.
